From dcee2e40d6f0e432cb07e19c4244094de024bd2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 15:03:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Batch the L-expanded density and its gradient in
 calExchangeJl (LDA + GGA)

Squashed combination of the two MuST_claude_GGA change sets:

  1. GPU-accelerate the L-expanded density interpolation in calExchangeJl
  2. Link DensityOnGridModule into the KUBO executable
  3. GGA: batch the density gradient on the same GPU/DGEMM path

Set (2) supersedes (1): the value-only interpolation of (1) is contained
verbatim in the GGA series as its first commit, so nothing from the
LDA-only change set is lost or duplicated here.

WHAT WAS SLOW

PotentialGenerationModule::calExchangeJl evaluated the charge and moment
density one point at a time via ChargeDensityModule::getChargeDensityAtPoint.
Each call repeated a hunt() bisection over the radial mesh plus an n_inter
point Neville interpolation for every jl component, even though the
evaluation points r_i*u_g lie exactly on radial mesh nodes and the angular
directions are fixed for the whole run. With n_r ~ 1500 and n_g = 50*80 that
is ~6e6 interpolations per (atom, species) per SCF iteration -- the dominant
full-potential cost, which the routine's own tc1 timer already exposed.

THE RESTRUCTURING -- VALUE

Radial interpolation is a linear operator on the stored coefficients, so it
separates from the angular sum:

    A(i,jl)      = sum_k w_k(r_i) * den_l(irp(i)+k-1, jl)
    rho(r_i,u_g) = sum_jl fa2(jl) * Re[ A(i,jl) * Y_jl(u_g) ]
                 = [Ar*Wr - Ai*Wi](i,g)

Wr(jl,g) = fa2(jl)*Re[Y_jl(u_g)], Wi likewise -- built once, since the
angular grid never changes. The angular sum is then two real GEMMs.

Lagrange weights replace the Neville recursion: same interpolating
polynomial, but linear in the data, which is what allows the factorization.
For on-node targets one numerator factor is exactly 0.0 and the surviving
ratio is a quotient of two identical products, i.e. exactly 1.0, so the
result is bitwise identical to the previous path there.

THE RESTRUCTURING -- GRADIENT

The gradient factorizes the same way. In
SphericalHarmonicsModule::SphericalHarmonics4 the gradient scaling is
rfac = clm(jl)/r, with the theta/phi unit vectors depending on direction
only, so the radius enters grad_ylm only as an overall 1/r and the angular
part can be tabulated once on the unit sphere, exactly like Y_jl itself.
With Adot(i,jl) the interpolated radial derivatives:

    den_grid(i,g) = sum_jl fa2(jl) * Re[ A(i,jl)    * Y_jl(u_g)   ]
    D(i,g)        = sum_jl fa2(jl) * Re[ Adot(i,jl) * Y_jl(u_g)   ]
    T_c(i,g)      = sum_jl fa2(jl) * Re[ A(i,jl)    * G_jl,c(u_g) ]

    grad_c(i,g)   = D(i,g)*u_c(g) + T_c(i,g)/r_i

with G_jl,c(u_g) = fa2(jl) * [r * d/dx_c Y_jl] evaluated at r = 1. This
reproduces term by term the CPU expression in getChargeDensityAtPoint,

    grad(i) += fa2(jl)*Re[ der_rho_in*ylm(kl)*er(i)
                           + rho_in(jl)*grad_ylm(kl,i) ]

with er = u_g. D reuses the existing Wr/Wi tables (one extra GEMM pair);
T_c needs three new tables Gr(:,:,c), Gi(:,:,c), built once, and three more
GEMM pairs. Total eight GEMM pairs per (atom, species) instead of two,
versus roughly n_r * n_g * jmax Neville interpolations before.

NEW FILES

  MST/Accelerator/DensityInterp_Accel.cu
      CUDA kernels (device bracket search + Lagrange weights, strided
      L-component interpolation, Ylm and grad-Ylm weight packing, gradient
      assembly T_c <- D*u_c + T_c/r) plus the cublasDgemm calls. extern "C"
      entry points use the trailing-underscore, by-pointer convention of
      LSMS_Accel.cu; own stream and cuBLAS handle; rank-modulo-device
      assignment as in init_lsms_gpu.
  MST/src/DensityOnGridModule.F90
      Driver with a CPU BLAS-DGEMM path algebraically identical to the GPU
      path, so the restructuring pays off with ACCEL off and the two paths
      can be cross-checked against each other. Provides
      calDensityOnAngularGrid, calDensityGradOnAngularGrid and the
      isDensityGradAvailable() guard.

MODIFIED FILES

  MST/src/PotentialGenerationModule.F90
      calExchangeJl batch-evaluates the density (and, for GGA, its gradient)
      before the point loop; init/teardown of the new module;
      initDensityOnGrid(..., needGrad = gga_functional) and
      use_batched = (.not.gga_functional) .or. isDensityGradAvailable().
  MST/src/AngularIntegrationModule.F90
      expose getYlmTable / getUnitVecTable (+ target attributes).
  MST/Accelerator/Makefile        add DensityInterp_Accel.o to OBJ0
  MST/Accelerator/CMakeLists.txt  add DensityInterp_Accel.cu
  MST/src/Makefile                add DensityOnGridModule.o
  KUBO/src/Makefile               link DensityOnGridModule into KUBO

The exchange-correlation evaluation itself stays on the CPU, single
threaded; only the interpolation was offloaded. The per-point fallback is
retained and is taken whenever isDensityGradAvailable() is false, so no
configuration silently loses its gradient.

NOT COMPILE-VERIFIED. The authoring environment had neither gfortran nor
nvcc. Verification was structural only.
